legality='The fixed helper mirrors N=5,F=2 constructor defaults and buffers actual SendMsg bytes, with all messages decoded via the registered New/Unmarshal factory. collect performs peer receipt/decoding and creates receiver message-type queues; deliver asserts FIFO within each receiver/type before dispatching the actual handler. This preserves the layer implemented by S-receive and S-dispatch: different RPC types enter different channels, so pending Commit0 need not dispatch before later PreAccept1. Original row0.instance0 is fast-committed by actual replies from peers1/2, then delivered to peer2. One peer1 crash is represented by making Alive[1]=false at all live nodes and never scheduling it again; peer loss updates that eligibility in S-receive. No new peer order or messages are fabricated. Thrifty failover sends next row0.instance1 to peers2/3. Node3 receives it while old Commit0 remains queued, so crtInstance[0]=1 with missing slot0, checked before the actual executeCommands loop starts. All protocol states are quiescent during the timer loop. It emits the real instancesToRecover request after its own counter; the harness checks row/instance identity and dispatches startRecoveryForInstance as run does. The queue is unbuffered solely to suspend the executor on its next instance1 request. This is a scheduling refinement of the original buffered producer, which may be suspended at the same send point; it does not change which first request was produced or the protocol state. Between sends for instance0 and instance1 the executor only increments k and reads unchanged crtInstance; it cannot read/write instance state while the recovery handlers run. The fixed handler suffix obtains a quorum from peers2/4, then dispatches the emitted phase1 and Accept exchanges with peers4/0. All decisions derive from target handlers, not installed snapshots. At the fixed observation point node3 live state has no concurrent executor access. Cleanup sets Shutdown only after observation, receives the next instance1 request, then joins the executor; the unbuffered send/receive orders the Shutdown write before the next loop read. Cleanup is independent of the compared result. Pending Commit and other messages remain visible and no global quiescence or eventual progress is assumed.'
